oday, some sections of the massive walls of the ancient fortress remain. The fortress, which was built in the 16th century, was once one of the most secure strongholds in France.","wpu":"Cimetière du Château","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Cimeti%C3%A8re_du_ch%C3%A2teau_Nice_1.jpg/960px-Cimeti%C3%A8re_du_ch%C3%A2teau_Nice_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Cimiez Cathedral","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.695833,"lo":7.281111,"d":2568,"ds":"Cimiez Cathedral (French: Cathédrale Sainte-Marie de Cimiez, also Cathédrale Notre-Dame du Château) was a Roman Catholic church in the southern town of Nice, France. The cathedral sat on the hill of the Château de Nice, a castle overlooking the city. The bishop's seat was transferred to the present Nice Cathedral in 1590.","wpu":"Cimiez Cathedral","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Vestiges_de_l%27ancienne_cath%C3%A9drale_de_Nice.jpg/960px-Vestiges_de_l%27ancienne_cath%C3%A9drale_de_Nice.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Musée d'art moderne et d'art contemporain","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.7014,"lo":7.2785,"d":2628,"ds":"The Musée d'art moderne et d'art contemporain (Museum of Modern and Contemporary Art), also known as MAMAC, is a museum dedicated to modern art and contemporary art. It opened on 21 June 1990, in Nice, France.","wpu":"Musée d'art moderne et d'art contemporain","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/5d/Muzeum_um%C4%9Bn%C3%AD_Nice.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Monument to the Dead of Rauba-Capeu","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.69376,"lo":7.28104,"d":2664,"ds":"The Monument to the Dead of Rauba-Capeù is located at the quay Rauba-Capeù along the tourist Seaside Road (Route du bord de mer) in Nice, France. The quay takes its name from the section of the promenade des Anglais near the monument, where the wind is said to be so strong as to \"steal one's cap\" (rauba capeù in the dialect of niçois).","wpu":"Monument to the Dead of Rauba-Capeu","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Nice_-_panoramio_-_Alistair_Cunningham_%283%29.jpg/960px-Nice_-_panoramio_-_Alistair_Cunningham_%283%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Palais Lascaris","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.6977,"lo":7.2773,"d":2798,"ds":"The Palais Lascaris is a seventeenth-century aristocratic building in Nice, France. Currently, it is a musical instrument museum. Located in the old town of Nice, it houses a collection of over 500 instruments, which makes it France’s second most important collection after the Musée de la Musique de la Philharmonie in Paris.","wpu":"Palais Lascaris","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Room_in_Palais_Lascaris%2C_Nice.JPG/960px-Room_in_Palais_Lascaris%2C_Nice.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Nice Cathedral","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.697222,"lo":7.275833,"d":2925,"ds":"Nice Cathedral (French: Basilique-Cathédrale Sainte-Marie et Sainte-Réparate de Nice) is a Catholic cathedral located in the city of Nice in southern France. The cathedral is the seat of the Diocese of Nice. The construction of the main part of the current cathedral's body started in 1650 and finished in 1685.","wpu":"Nice Cathedral","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Cathedrale_Sainte-Reparate_Nice.jpg/960px-Cathedrale_Sainte-Reparate_Nice.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Palais Hongran","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.6956,"lo":7.273,"d":3194,"ds":"The Palais Hongran is a historic mansion in Nice, Alpes-Maritimes, France. It was built from 1769 to 1772. It has been listed as an official national monument since December 2, 2010.","wpu":"Palais Hongran","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Palais_Hongran_01.jpg/960px-Palais_Hongran_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Abbey of Saint-Pons de Nice","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.725,"lo":7.2832,"d":3231,"ds":"The Abbey of Saint Pons (French: Abbaye Saint-Pons de Nice) is one of the oldest monasteries on the French Riviera, along with Lérins Abbey. It is located in the municipality of Nice in the Alpes-Maritimes. The original abbey was constructed between 774 and 800 and entrusted to the Benedictines.","wpu":"Abbey of Saint-Pons de Nice","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Nice%2C_France%2C_Abbaye_de_Saint_Pons%2C_in_the_valley.jpg/960px-Nice%2C_France%2C_Abbaye_de_Saint_Pons%2C_in_the_valley.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Musée Matisse (Nice)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.719444,"lo":7.276111,"d":3288,"ds":"The Musée Matisse (French: [myze matis]) in Nice is a municipal museum devoted to the work of French painter Henri Matisse. It gathers one of the world's largest collections of his works, tracing his artistic beginnings and his evolution through his last works. The museum, which opened in 1963, is located in the Villa des Arènes, a seventeenth-century villa in the neighborhood of Cimiez.","wpu":"Musée Matisse (Nice)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Mus%C3%A9e_Matisse_Nice.JPG/960px-Mus%C3%A9e_Matisse_Nice.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Neuf Lignes Obliques","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.695325,"lo":7.271147,"d":3345,"ds":"Neuf lignes obliques (English: Nine Oblique Lines) is a steel monument on the Promenade des Anglais, by French artist Bernar Venet. It was commissioned to mark the 150th anniversary of the 1860 annexation of the County of Nice by France. The sculpture is made of nine steel beams, 30 metres long, which meet at their top.","wpu":"Neuf Lignes Obliques","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Neuf_Lignes_Obliques_Nice_IMG_1246.jpg/960px-Neuf_Lignes_Obliques_Nice_IMG_1246.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Place Masséna","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.6973,"lo":7.2701,"d":3371,"ds":"The Place Masséna (French pronunciation: [plas masena]) is a historic square in Nice, Alpes-Maritimes, France. It was named for André Masséna. Its layout was designed by Joseph Vernier in 1843-1844.","wpu":"Place Masséna","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Place-Mass%C3%A9na_01.jpg/960px-Place-Mass%C3%A9na_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Nice Synagogue","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.6998,"lo":7.26935,"d":3380,"ds":"The Nice Synagogue (French: Synagogue de Nice), also the Great Synagogue in Nice, is an Orthodox Jewish congregation and synagogue, located at 7, rue Gustave Deloye in Nice, Alpes-Maritimes, in the Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur region of France. Designed by Paul Martin, the synagogue was built in 1885, and dedicated in 1886. It was listed as a monument historique on April 17, 2004.","wpu":"Nice Synagogue","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Nice_Synagogue.jpg/960px-Nice_Synagogue.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Musée Marc Chagall","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.709167,"lo":7.269536,"d":3382,"ds":"The Marc Chagall National Museum (Chagall Biblical Message) is a French national museum dedicated to the work of painter Marc Chagall – particularly his works inspired by religion – located in Nice in the Alpes-Maritimes.","wpu":"Musée Marc Chagall","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/08/Mus%C3%A9e_Marc_Chagall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Saint Nicholas and Saint Alexandra Church","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.699386,"lo":7.267608,"d":3525,"ds":"The Saint Nicholas and Saint Alexandra Church (French: Église Saint-Nicolas-et-Sainte-Alexandra) or Saints Nicholas and Alexandra Church (French: Église Saints-Nicolas-et-Alexandra) is an orthodox church in Nice (Alpes-Maritimes), on rue Longchamp. It is dedicated to Saint Nicholas and Saint Alexandra.","wpu":"Saint Nicholas and Saint Alexandra Church","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Eglise_russe_longchamp_nice.jpg/960px-Eglise_russe_longchamp_nice.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Théâtre de Verdure de Nice","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.6957,"lo":7.2678,"d":3593,"ds":"The Théâtre de Verdure de Nice (French pronunciation: [teɑtʁ də vɛʁdyʁ də nis]) is an outdoor theater located in Nice, France, which was built in 1946 by architect François Aragon. It has a capacity of 1,850 seated or 3,200 standing.","wpu":"Théâtre de Verdure de Nice","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/87/Th%C3%A9%C3%A2tre_de_verdure_de_Nice.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Notre-Dame de Nice","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.703311,"lo":7.266061,"d":3613,"ds":"The Basilica of Notre-Dame de Nice (French: Basilique Notre-Dame de Nice) is a Roman Catholic basilica situated on the Avenue Jean Médecin in the centre of Nice, in France. It is built in the Neo-Gothic architectural tradition. The basilica, built between 1864 and 1868, was designed by Louis Lenormand and is the largest church in Nice, but is not the cathedral of the city.","wpu":"Notre-Dame de Nice","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Notre-Dame_de_Nice_PC250048_%2850551243457%29.jpg/960px-Notre-Dame_de_Nice_PC250048_%2850551243457%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Palais de la Méditerranée","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.695239,"lo":7.263131,"d":3969,"ds":"The Palais de la Méditerranée, now know as Hôtel Palais de la Méditerranée, is a luxury hotel located at 13 Promenade des Anglais in Nice, France. Part of The Unbound Collection by Hyatt, the hotel is housed in an iconic Art Deco building constructed in 1928 and inaugurated in 1929. It is owned by Constellation Hotels Holding and operated by the international hotel group Hyatt Hotels.","wpu":"Palais de la Méditerranée","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Palais_de_la_m%C3%A9dit%C3%A9rran%C3%A9e.jpg/960px-Palais_de_la_m%C3%A9dit%C3%A9rran%C3%A9e.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sainte Jeanne d'Arc Church, Nice","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.713411,"lo":7.2626,"d":4028,"ds":"The Church of Saint Joan of Arc (French: Sainte Jeanne d'Arc) is a Roman Catholic parish church located in Nice, France. Noticeable for its original architecture, the church is dedicated to Joan of Arc. The style of this church is controversial among the inhabitants of Nice, judged ugly by some.","wpu":"Sainte Jeanne d'Arc Church, Nice","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/0/04/Saint_Joan_of_Arc_-_Nice_-_France.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"}],"about":{"ds":"Nice is a city and commune, and the prefecture of the department of Alpes-Maritimes, and the administrative region of Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur, France. It is located on the French Riviera, the country's southeastern coast on the Mediterranean Sea, at the foot of the French Alps. It was the capital city of the historical County of Nice (French: Comté de Nice, Niçard: Countèa de Nissa).","wpu":"Nice","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Promenade_des_Anglais_Nice_IMG_1255.jpg/960px-Promenade_des_Anglais_Nice_IMG_1255.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}